
  [38;5;153mcore.js[39m exports a [38;5;153mcreateLogger({ sink, level, name })[39m factory rather than a singleton, so tests can construct one with
  a fake sink and no global state. Its last line is the dual-mode footer that makes the same file work under [38;5;153mrequire()[39m
  and under a [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag.

  Wiring: [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gains three script tags before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m ([38;5;153mredact.js[39m, [38;5;153mcore.js[39m, [38;5;153msink-browser.js[39m) — order matters and
  I'll comment it. [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m requires the core and the Node sink and builds its logger at startup.

  The four existing [38;5;153mconsole.*[39m calls get replaced, not supplemented: [38;5;153mapp.js:5[39m and [38;5;153mapp.js:24[39m are the ones currently
  sitting in the password's scope, and leaving them alongside a redacting logger would mean the redaction is decorative.
  [38;5;153msrc/index.js:4[39m is a genuine program output rather than a diagnostic, so that one stays a [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m — logging and
  printing aren't the same job.

  [1mTooling.[0m The repo has no linter, formatter, test runner, or scripts, and it's cheapest to set these up before the code
  exists rather than after. My suggestion: ESLint + Prettier, and [38;5;153mnode:test[39m as the runner — it's built into Node, so
  the repo stays zero-dependency for tests, which matters for a project that currently has no [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m at all. E2E
  (Playwright) I'd skip; there's one form and the cost is disproportionate. Property-based testing I'd normally skip
  too, except the redactor is an unusually good fit for it — it has a simple invariant ("no denied field ever appears in
  output") that's much better checked by generated input than by examples.
